articles.body:
John Ternus didn’t mention Apple Home once during his first keynote as Apple CEO. But he did open by describing an “intelligent personal hub” that prioritizes local processing, protects your privacy, and integrates with all your other devices, apps, and services. While Ternus was talking about the iPhone — not a new smart home device — what he spelled out is exactly what I want to see Apple make: an intelligent home hub, a computer for your home.
Rumors suggest Apple’s first smart home hub with a screen is imminent. While that’s long overdue, it’s likely to be more interface than infrastructure, a device that can run your home locally but still uses the cloud. What the smart home needs is a computer to run it — one that controls and processes data from connected devices locally, improving speed, reliability, and privacy while securely storing your digital life, from photos and files to music and movies. Crucially, it would do all this inside your home.&nbsp;
The cloud has worked well for years, but the recent proliferation of AI-powered home features, such as generating text descriptions from security camera footage, makes local computing more compelling. On-device processing can be faster and more private, and it depends less on an internet connection. These features will also evolve quickly to feed more context to our smart homes, bringing more utility but also a greater need for privacy and security.&nbsp;
[Image: The category of smart home super hubs emerged at IFA in Berlin this month. This is the Anker Eufy MindBase, a smart home hub that supports dual-band Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Thread, and Zigbee, and is a Matter controller. It runs an on-device, Anker-developed LLM with support for 16 wireless cameras with expandable local storage up to 48 TB. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/LR6B5731.jpeg?quality=90&strip=all]
This type of dedicated home computer — or smart home brain — was an emerging category at last week’s IFA consumer tech show. Anker launched its Eufy MindBase home hub, and Ugreen debuted its HomeAgent, both combining network-attached storage, local AI to process and store footage from cameras, with a smart home hub to connect all your devices.&nbsp;
But one piece is still missing: networking.&nbsp;
Most home routers haven’t caught up with today’s smart home. Despite years of talk about smarter routers, manufacturers and ISPs still seem reluctant to take on the complexity. Comcast has had Thread and Matter support in its gateways since 2022, but hasn’t turned them on. Connecting devices to your home network remains one of the most frustrating parts of the smart home, with manufacturers, ISPs, and platforms all pointing fingers when things fail. Ikea’s recent troubles rolling out new Matter-over-Thread devices are just one example.
Here’s where Apple could step in and combine all three — a smart home hub, local storage with AI processing capabilities, and a home router — into one device. A hub. AI. A router. Are you getting it? Call it an Apple Home Server.&nbsp;
There’s no evidence Apple is building such a thing. But the company’s recent contributions to OpenWrt, the open-source software that powers most routers, could be a tentative first step.
One Apple box for your home: Connect it to your modem, and it becomes your Wi-Fi router, Matter Controller, Thread border router, smart home hub, private AI computer, and home server. It stores your files, photos, music, and movies; processes your camera footage locally; runs your automations even when the internet is down; and optionally backs everything up to iCloud. It wouldn’t be another smart home hub; it would be the computer your home needs. Plug it in, sign in with your Apple account, and it just works.&nbsp;
That last part is crucial, and plays directly to Apple’s strength: hardware that is “super easy to use without a big learning curve,” as Ternus put it this week. Apple also has experience combining these pieces. AirPort Express put networking and media streaming on one box; AirPort Extreme and Time Capsule added NAS capabilities; and HomeKit Secure Routers brought Apple’s security into the network itself.&nbsp;
Apple walked away from all these products in 2018, saying at the time it might return to the space if it found a meaningful way to progress routers further. The pieces have never fit together more perfectly than they do now.&nbsp;
[Image: Apple’s Airport Express is a much-missed device. Resurrecting it as part of a powerful whole-home computer for today’s smart home setups would be a popular move. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/airport-extreme-hero.1419969698.webp?quality=90&strip=all]
Apple is deeply involved in developing Matter and is spearheading the Thread networking protocol. An Apple Home Server could bring the entire smart home stack together, letting Apple control everything end to end and remove much of the complexity. Matter even has a blueprint for part of this device: the Matter Home Router Access Point device type combines Wi-Fi networking and Thread Border Router functionality, and Apple’s contributions to OpenWrt focus on HRAP implementation.&nbsp;
According to reporting by Matter Alpha, Apple’s code includes production-grade networking infrastructure that could be turned into a consumer product. There’s no evidence that’s what Apple is doing; it could simply be for router manufacturers. But the pieces are falling into place. With Apple bringing Apple Intelligence to its HomeKit Secure Video service in iOS 27, a Home Server device could take that service to the next level with fully local processing.
Local is the key. An Apple Home Server would be a supercharged Apple Home Hub, powering local-first smart home automations, keeping data private and secure at home, and improving responsiveness for devices like smart lights, locks, and security cameras. It could work alongside existing home hubs — the HomePod, Apple TV, and rumored HomePad — for voice and screen interfaces.&nbsp;
This might go against the grain. Apple loves subscriptions. But it loves its premium hardware even more, and this would undoubtedly be premium hardware. Also, a device like this would give Apple a powerful foundation for everything it does in the home: iPhones, Macs, iPads, Apple TVs, HomePods, its existing digital services, and potential future AI-powered ones. And, unlike a typical smart home hub, it could appeal to anyone who wants a reliable, private home router and a way to store their digital life at home.&nbsp;
Smart home infrastructure is broken, and Apple is uniquely positioned to fix it. An Apple Home Server is the hardware backbone Apple Home has always needed. As our digital lives move deeper into the cloud and AI makes the smart home more dependent on data, the case for a computer to run our homes gets stronger, and building one on open standards could limit lock-in.&nbsp;
Apple has all the pieces. The smart home has been waiting for someone to put them together. If Apple finally builds it, the only surprise will be that it took this long.

Arizona, an epicenter for the bipartisan push to revive American chip manufacturing, is about to lose more than a quarter of the water it typically pulls each year from the Colorado River.&nbsp;
The 1,400-mile waterway is a crucial lifeline for the region and other western states. A recent federal decision on how to manage its dwindling flow hits Arizona, and its already drought-prone desert communities, particularly hard. The river provides more than a third of the Grand Canyon State’s water. Arizona has been a manufacturing hub since the start of the semiconductor industry, and now it’s where TSMC and Intel are expanding massive factories to make advanced chips for AI — a water-intensive effort.&nbsp;
City planners and industry leaders have already taken precautions to conserve water and find alternative sources. It’s a resource that is likely to become more expensive for manufacturers and residents alike as they learn to rely less on the Colorado River. That’s already raising tensions over who shoulders the costs for new infrastructure needed to adapt, as well as what constitutes a good use of water. The challenges that come with managing a limited water supply are only going to grow as AI demands a larger share.&nbsp;
“Our economy is changing, and that’s really painful,” says Kathryn Sorensen, research director at the Kyl Center for Water Policy at Arizona State University. “When there’s a water scarcity issue you have to start looking at what the uses of that water are that bring the most value to the community.”
Agriculture has been a major part of Arizona’s economy since it became a state, and still gobbles up more than 70 percent of the state’s water. Industrial uses, including the chip manufacturing that’s had a foothold in the state since the 1940s, account for just 6 percent.&nbsp;
While the amount of water that semiconductor factories, or fabs, use is still quite small compared to agriculture, the chip industry is growing fast. Fabs use “ultra-pure” water to clean surfaces and etch sensitive chips. It takes more municipal water to create a smaller amount of ultra-pure water through deionization and reverse osmosis. A typical facility might use 10 million gallons of ultra-pure water a day, which could require up to 16 million gallons of municipal water.
New chip factories are sprouting up in Arizona’s desert with the help of federal funding. President Joe Biden signed the ​​CHIPS and Science Act into law in 2022, setting aside $52.7 billion to expand domestic chip manufacturing. Armed with a $7.86 billion grant from the government, Intel is planning to spend $32 billion on two new factories and an expansion of an existing facility in Arizona. In 2025, the company’s facilities in Chandler, Arizona withdrew more than 2.7 billion gallons of freshwater, according to its latest corporate responsibility report.
TSMC, the world’s largest chipmaker, received up to $6.6 billion in CHIPS Act funding and decided to build at least six new semiconductor fabs in Phoenix. TSMC’s first fab in Arizona uses roughly 4.75 million gallons of water a day, the company told The Verge last year.&nbsp;It’s approximately equivalent to the water usage of more than 14,000 homes a day, or 2 percent of Phoenix’s housing. That amount grows with each new fab the company builds, and with the more advanced manufacturing processes TSMC is developing. The company recycles 65 percent of the water it uses in Arizona, TSMC says, a figure that’s supposed to grow to at least 90 percent when it finishes building a water reclamation plant.&nbsp;
The Colorado River is the state’s biggest renewable source of water. But the Colorado River Basin has faced “unprecedented” drought for more than a quarter century, according to the US Bureau of Reclamation. Climate change, unfortunately, is also making the region more dry as growing water demand adds to decades of overuse.
“There’s not enough water for everything we want to do with it,” Sorensen, who previously worked as the director of Phoenix Water Services, says. “So what do we have enough water for? What are the uses we most value? Is it cotton or is it microchips?”
With 40 million Americans from Denver to Los Angeles relying on water from the Colorado River, states have been tussling over how to allocate the remaining water for years. Guidelines for how to manage that water, collectively known as the “Law of the River,” expire this year. After states failed to reach an agreement on their own, the US Department of the Interior finalized its own plan in August for how to move forward. It outlines steep cuts for downriver states: Arizona, Nevada, and California. Arizona stands to lose the most, at least 760,000 acre-feet of water a year starting in 2027. An acre-foot represents how much water it would take to cover an acre with a foot of water, and is enough to supply about three homes with water for a year in the Phoenix area. The City of Phoenix uses about 300,000 acre feet of water in a year.&nbsp;
Maricopa County, home to Intel and TSMC’s chip fabs, isn’t as reliant on water from the Colorado River as other counties. Its primary resource is groundwater, but surface water flowing from the Colorado and Salt rivers still make up close to 40 percent of the county’s supply.&nbsp;
Local leaders insist that Phoenix and other cities in the region are prepared for water cuts, citing plans to make up for the shortfall with underground water stores and other surface water sources. “For the most part, they’ve planned accordingly and can fall back onto water that was previously stored underground,” Sorensen says.&nbsp;
But even without sudden tap water shortages, Sorensen expects utility bills to climb over time. The transition to alternative water resources will entail new infrastructure, like new wells and pipes. That’s likely to be the most significant driver of higher tap water prices, Sorensen explains.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, the city of Phoenix is pushing back, with Mayor Kate Gallego arguing that the federal government’s decision to cut the state’s intake “does not provide the fair, basin-wide leadership the Colorado River crisis demands.” That said, “Phoenix is prepared for this moment because of decades of planning and conservation, we will push for new federal support to mitigate the rising cost of water that will result from this federal decision,” Gallego added in an August press release. A spokesperson for the city declined to comment further in an interview.
TSMC in particular, located in Phoenix, will be subject to the same price hikes as its neighbors. Phoenix is unique in that it sets the same water rates for companies as it does for residential customers. Cities often charge industrial customers less if they want to attract certain businesses.&nbsp;
“The cost of water will increase for TSMC,” Sorensen says. “Everyone pays the same rate for water in the city of Phoenix, whether you’re a mom-and-pop store, a family of eight, or TSMC.”
TSMC and Intel declined interviews with The Verge for this story. Both have made public commitments, however, to minimize the amount of fresh water they consume.&nbsp;
“A stable supply of water is critical to chip production,” TSMC deputy spokesperson Nina Kao said in an emailed statement. “One of the reasons the city of Phoenix was selected for our US expansion is because of their long-term water plan, which includes varied water supply sources, as well as steps taken in preparation for reduced water supplies over the course of decades.”&nbsp;
Apprehensions are still mounting over the impact that AI and the tech industry as a whole have on the region. AI has spawned a new generation of semiconductor factories, chip packaging plants, and data centers across Arizona.&nbsp;
“It’s almost happening at a level that’s difficult to wrap your head around,” says Jennifer Martin-McLeod, a program manager for the Grand Canyon chapter of Sierra Club. “To try to say ‘Everybody take a breath and think through what we’re doing’ is difficult when you have that steamroller of money coming at your state to try to make it all happen overnight.”
She has broader concerns about the health of the Colorado River and wildlife that depends on it. Over the long term, Sorensen points out that a shortage of water from the Colorado River will result in the depletion of groundwater in Central Arizona, as most cities cope by increasing how much water they draw from local aquifers. “It’s something they can sustain, but it is only sustained at a cost to future generations,” she adds.&nbsp;
The impact of water cuts next year will be felt most by typical residents, Martin-McLeod says. “For me, that’s going to mean serious lifestyle changes. … But to an enormous chip manufacturer, that’s maybe just part of the cost of doing business, you know?”

Apple was far from the first company to announce a passport-shaped folding phone this year, but that doesn’t mean it wasn’t copied. As my colleague&nbsp;Dom Preston already explained, it’s almost impossible to keep product development secret when Apple and its competitors all share the same global suppliers.&nbsp;
Apple’s new CEO John Ternus and head of marketing Greg ‘Joz’ Joswiak addressed the topic during an interview with TechRadar’s Lance Ulanoff and Mark Spoonauer of Tom’s Guide.
“People knew through leaks that we were working on a foldable,” said Joz. “Unfortunately, they got the aspect ratio, and a lot of coincidental instances of people finding that an interesting aspect ratio.”&nbsp;By “people,” Joz means&nbsp;Samsung,&nbsp;Huawei,&nbsp;and&nbsp;Xiaomi who all announced wide foldables ahead of Apple.
What did remain a secret is the folding nano-texture display unique to the iPhone Duo.&nbsp;“What I’m super proud of — we kept it secret,” said Joz. “We put the extra security on it. We didn’t talk about it to anybody.”&nbsp;
The whole interview is worth a watch. Ternus’ boyish enthusiasm is a refreshing change from the more measured Tim Cook, signaling his roots as a hands-on engineer. You can see it when describing his first interaction with a prototype of the display. “Oh this is it, this is what I want,” he said, eagerly grinning.
The new head of Apple is clearly a product guy, just like its mercurial founder.

A simple way for an untrusted site to freeze a Mac
A WebGPU shader on an untrusted site can hang a Mac's graphics, making the desktop UI unusable until a restart is forced. All the victim needs to do is click a link. This issue reproduces cross-browser on MacOS, but not other OSes.
THE BELOW BUTTON WILL FREEZE YOUR MAC. IT MAY SLOW OTHER OSes
On other OSes I've tested, it causes the tab to freeze and things to chug but once you close the tab you're fine. But your mileage may vary! Only click the above if you're willing to freeze your computer.
I've reproduced the deathray under MacOS on Chrome, Firefox, and Safari. It does not reproduce under other operating systems. I've only tried testing on M-series Macbooks running Tahoe; I'd be interested to hear if other Macs are impacted too!
The deathray uses a relatively new web technology: WebGPU. WebGPU provides built-in browser APIs that sites can use to submit shaders to run on the device's GPU. It's meant to replace WebGL and is supported across all major browsers on most OSes.
The entire deathray fits in a single small file. You can see the source code here. Most of the file is scaffolding; the important part is the WebGPU shaders.
@group(0) @binding(0) var<storage, read_write> data : array<vec4f>;
@compute @workgroup_size(1) fn compute() {
// no i++, loop spins endlessly
for(var i = 0u; i < 1;) {
@group(0) @binding(0) var<storage, read> data : array<vec4f>;
@vertex fn vert(@builtin(vertex_index) vertexIdx : u32)
// attempts to read from same buffer the compute shader writes to
@fragment fn frag(@builtin(position) pos : vec4f)
In the compute shader an infinite busy loop copies the same vector over and over again in a buffer. The vertex shader depends on the same data buffer the compute shader is operating on. Because the compute shader is busy looping, the vertex shader can't move forward.
This spills over into other processes wanting to use the GPU, namely the WindowServer. As a result of our WebGPU pileup, the WindowServer becomes unresponsive as well. This doesn't always manifest the same way. Sometimes I can still move the mouse, sometimes not. Sometimes I get the beachball, and sometimes magenta junk shows up on part of the screen. I'm not sure what leads to the different behavior!
Interestingly, the rest of the computer is fine at this point. You can SSH into the computer without issue. But there's a Watchdog checking the WindowServer. When it doesn't respond for long enough, it triggers a kernel panic and the computer restarts. If you've been deathray'd and are impatient you can hold down the power button to turn off and restart without waiting for the Watchdog. Just hope that your browser doesn't automatically reopen the same tab when it starts up again :)
This is actually not the first time that Apple has had this problem. In 2023, Ron Masas of Imperva crafted a similar malicious shader named ShadyShader that uses WebGL. ShadyShader also uses a runaway loop to hog the GPU, crafting enormous nested loops that are technically non-infinite.
Apple responded by issuing CVE-2023-40441 with a 6.5 (medium) CVSS score. To mitigate the problem, Apple added improved input validation to better detect runaway loops.
That input validation seems to be weaker in WebGPU - the deathray has a trivially identifiable infinite loop. But ultimately detecting infinite loops is a losing game; you can't get around the halting problem. There needs to be better pre-emption of unresponsive shaders, especially if those shaders are running untrusted code. All other operating systems I tested the deathray on get this right.
I wonder if part of the challenge Apple faces in fixing these type of issues is due to the architecture of M-series chips. The OS kernel cannot directly pre-empt the GPU. Instead, GPU handling is performed by a coprocessor known as the ASC which the OS kernel communicates with. All the GPU pre-emption logic lives in the ASC firmware. Asahi Lina has an excellent writeup on the M-series GPUs that explores the architecture in more detail.
How was the deathray found?
I found this issue the old fashioned way: by accident! I was learning WebGPU at the Recurse Center and accidentally made an infinite loop. I was surprised when it hung my computer and of course asked "Does it do that every time?" Sure enough it does! I can't be the first person to have tripped across this.
The issue was disclosed to Apple Security on 7/27/26. Apple quickly reproduced the issue and stated that they intended to fix it. They gave a fix timeline which was marked as confidential, so I will not write it here.
However, on 8/26/26 Apple changed tack, saying that they did "not see any security implications with [my] report" and that the report did not "result in a change in [Apple's] products." They said the report would be sent to another team for "potential enhancement considerations." To me that sounds like it will be a very low priority to fix, if it is ever addressed. It feels a bit curious given how ShadyShader was marked as a 6.5 medium severity issue.
That being said, I think there's an interesting discussion to be had about what constitutes a security issue. Security researchers I've talked with tend to agree with Apple. As Apple puts it, "the result is either a crash, hang, or recoverable data loss which we do not consider to be a security issue." And on a technical level I don't think they're wrong.
However, the majority of non-security people I've talked to are surprised with that characterization and that Apple is not prioritizing fixing the issue. I think among most folks there's a pretty strong trust in the browser. There's a (perhaps misplaced) belief that simply clicking on a link will not negatively impact the rest of your computer beyond maybe slowing things down until the tab is closed. Something like the deathray violates that trust in a very visible way, and I think that most users see that as a security issue.
What I hope to see
The deathray is certainly nowhere near as severe as a sandbox escape, RCE, or data breach. But given its extremely low barrier to entry (just get someone to click on a link) it has the potential to be quite annoying. If used maliciously, I see it as a meaner version of a Rickroll, and I hope Apple ends up fixing it before too long. (And Apple, please don't have the fix be disabling WebGPU by default! I've come to love WebGPU 💚).
But until Apple fixes it, I hope people find ways to have fun with it non-maliciously. I'd love to see deathray video games where if you lose, your Mac freezes (with the user opting into this ahead of time). A much milder version of "you die in the game, you die in real life."
Have fun cats and kittens, but please be responsible!

Whenever the algorithm serves up a steaming pile of looksmaxxing, I flash back to an old sketch from That Mitchell and Webb Look. An ordinary woman is told by a commercial narrator that she’s “leaking, hairy, aging, overweight, and everything hurts.” Women, the smug narrator says, for god’s sake, sort yourselves out by buying roughly $300 of products. After a jump cut, an average man is told to shave and get drunk, guitars blaring in the background. The reason? Because he’s already brilliant.
Back when that sketch first aired in 2009, it was funny because it felt broadly true. But in the age of looksmaxxing, where men are increasingly obsessed with testosterone, smashing their bones in pursuit of a sharp jawline, and optimizing their penises, that sketch feels like a glimpse into antiquity.&nbsp;
Today, you have men in subreddits and Facebook groups posting before-and-after pics at the gym, arms flexed for maximum veiny bicep effect. In the comments, they ask for each other’s stacks, protocols, and dosing advice, which include everything from T to unapproved gray-market peptides. Some say it’s to score baddies. Others say it’s to maximize their gains at the gym. A few seem to think turning themselves into science experiments is how they’ll find the fountain of youth. To me, it feels similar to what women have done for ages: buying snake oil that preys on their insecurities.&nbsp;
“I take Cialis, but not for sex,” begins one of biohacker and tech entrepreneur Bryan Johnson’s X posts. “It’s actually a longevity medicine. Cialis (Tadalafil) is great for the same reason it gives you fantastic erections… it improves blood flow.”
Tadalafil does, in fact, improve blood flow. The medicine is a vasodilator, meaning it relaxes muscles and widens blood vessels. It’s most widely known as a treatment for erectile dysfunction. (To be clear, it doesn’t cause erections but helps them last when they occur.) It’s also prescribed to alleviate urinary issues in people with enlarged prostates and high blood pressure in the pulmonary artery. Biohackers like Johnson tout a low dose of tadalafil daily as helping lower cardiovascular risk and cognitive decline. Gym rats often microdose tadalafil as a preworkout supplement because, well, the erections are supposedly better and dilating blood vessels is thought to lead to better “muscle pumps.”&nbsp;
Enhanced, the company behind the pro-doping Enhanced Games, sells low-dose tadalafil on its site. Clavicular, the most infamous looksmaxxer, has also stated that he’s taken about 5mg of tadalafil in the past as part of an extensive stack. This, despite the fact that he’s 20 years old and far from the conventional profile of someone a doctor might prescribe tadalafil to. Popular podcaster Andrew Huberman has also recommended on his show that every man over 40 should be taking a low dose of tadalafil as a preventative measure.&nbsp;
[Image: Cialis, or tadalafil, works by relaxing muscles and improving blood flow. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/gettyimages-151054882.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]
Of course, tadalafil is just like any other medicine. While many may not experience adverse effects at low daily dosages, some will. And like any medication, there are some people who shouldn’t take it and there are drug interactions to be wary of.&nbsp;
It’d be one thing if these influencers were just promoting tadalafil. But most are promoting multiple substances, seemingly all of which you need to be a man. Testosterone, for instance, is massively popular in the same cohort. Ninety-one percent of the athletes at the inaugural Enhanced Games dabbled with testosterone. Notably, Pete Hegseth recently announced mandatory testosterone screenings for the troops. Some also hype up retatrutide, an unapproved peptide currently undergoing phase three clinical trials, along with TB-500 and BPC-157, two gray-market peptides colloquially known as the Wolverine stack for their supposed wound healing and muscle recovery benefits. And the more things you stack, the greater the risk that a combination has an unintended effect. In the case of lesser-studied experimental treatments, even doctors may not know what those effects are — and might not know for a long time.&nbsp;
The stated reasons for taking these risks are myriad. But whether it’s because it’ll help someone get jacked, live longer, perform better in bed, or just “have more energy,” it boils down to a singular message: It’ll make you more of a man.
“This kind of masculinity is a little bit fantastical,” says Dr. Arthur Burnett, the Patrick C. Walsh professor of urology at Johns Hopkins, referring to the current cultural obsession with “maxxing.” Burnett should know. On top of over three decades as a men’s health expert, his research into nitric oxide biochemical mechanisms in erectile tissue helped pave the way for drugs like Viagra.&nbsp;
Regarding men taking on all of these additional requirements, he says that “there’s not really a scientific basis.” “I think it’s unfortunate that we’ve pushed the pendulum in a direction that may be harmful, frankly, psychologically at the very least but possibly even physically to the body. It could be problematic trying to adopt medical terms like ‘prevention’ and ‘men’s health’.”&nbsp;
Are you a peptide user?
I’m researching a story on peptide use, and I’m looking to interview regular people who are using gray-market peptides. If you’re interested in participating, email me at victoria.song@theverge.com or message me at Signal @vicmsong.14.
Burnett is essentially pointing to the divide between how clinicians and the biohacking / looksmaxxing communities view medicine. On the one side, you have medical professionals who largely view drugs as a way to treat deficiencies and bring people back to a “normal” baseline. On the other, you have influencers and enthusiasts (which can include clinicians) who believe that drugs can and ought to be used to “enhance” people beyond their natural limits. It’s a tension that’s at the heart of so many debates in the wellness wild west.&nbsp;
“Preventative care should really be something that those who are at risk or have health adversities can make changes in a way that’s going to correct a problem. What you have here is completely healthy people doing things that can be problematic,” explains Burnett.&nbsp;
Testosterone, he says, is a classic example. Otherwise healthy men who over-supplement the hormonal steroid often inadvertently make themselves infertile. For some men, too much testosterone can also trigger a genetic predisposition for hair thinning — which looksmaxxers often address by taking minoxidil. Add another supplement, drug, or product to combat the side effects of minoxidil — which include dizziness, headaches, and bloating — and that’s how you end up with a stack.
What gets lost in all these stacks and protocols is that health isn’t about optimization. For many young men, “optimizing” seems to be less of a goal than a vaguely defined baseline they’re expected to maintain forever. Anything less is a failure, a weakness — unhealthy.
A depressing way to spend an afternoon is rifling through looksmaxxing and gear forums. (I don’t recommend staying too long — unless you feel like torturing yourself reading 4chan-esque insults and slurs about penis size and “weekly slays.”)
In one post, a supposed 18-year-old asks for advice dosing Cialis to have better erections and “get more flaccid hang.” In a biohacking subreddit, a 30-year-old essentially crowdsources whether he’s developing a “dependency” on tadalafil. At no point, despite all the posturing, do any of the men in the posts I read seem to be genuinely happy. They’re often boastful about how good they feel or how many conquests they’ve had. But mostly, there seems to be an undercurrent of angry self-loathing. A desperate urge to “fix” oneself for… what or whom exactly? I never find a coherent answer.
[Image: I can’t believe I’m writing about Cialismaxxing. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/gettyimages-865564924.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]
Admittedly, I’m not a man. I will never know what it means to be a man or experience the societal pressures that men face. But there’s something strange about how the internet is amplifying a subsect of men turning themselves into science experiments. There’s a veritable pipeline from relatively benign trends like boy kibble protein-washing and news stories about RFK Jr. working out shirtless with Kid Rock to whatever it is the Enhanced Games and Bryan Johnson are doing. It’s easy to write off, until Whoop’s AI recommends your boss up his testosterone levels — not because he’s deficient, but because he’s not optimized for performance.&nbsp;
“It happens in my clinic periodically — guys thinking they need enhancements, or they’re unhappy with certain interventions, or they feel they’re not performing at a certain level,” says Burnett. “It’s counterproductive as it actually promotes stigma [around men’s health] because they can’t live up to what they think is the notion of the ideal man.” That, he says, creates more anxiety, stress, embarrassment, and shame. On top of that, it makes it hard for men to speak more openly about genuine health issues or seek a professional’s help. Anything “abnormal” becomes something to be suffered in silence or supplemented away.
For now, it’s probably contained to a chronically online niche. (Burnett says these behaviors aren’t heavily prevalent in his practice just yet, but then again — how likely are the most susceptible men to trust the medical establishment when that’s the first thing wellness grifters seek to discredit?) But this niche is loud and it loves to post. Eventually, those messages embed themselves in the broader culture — even if everyone knows better.
The only thing I can conclude is that the looksmaxxers are not okay. I hope that one day they will be, that this is a passing fad that one day we’ll look back and laugh at. But in the meantime, I’ll leave this with some advice from Burnett.
“As long as we’re living healthy, we’re doing the right thing. Please accept that you are a good man for who you are, and don’t be swayed by the influencers.”&nbsp;

Another researcher is challenging OpenAI about the data driving its increasingly impressive array of mathematical discoveries. Just days after a bitter row erupted over whether the company’s models benefited from unpublished work, a second mathematician has come forward accusing the AI giant of unethical and “dishonest” behavior and a lack of transparency about the origins of its training data.
In a series of posts on Mastodon, mathematician Andreas Thom raised concerns that interactions he and his colleagues had had with the ChatGPT chatbot before OpenAI’s triumphant announcement may have contributed to its success in the field. One of the 10 results OpenAI announced with great fanfare last month involved Thom’s area of expertise, so-called non-sofic groups, and OpenAI acknowledged that their result built heavily on previous work by Thom and fellow mathematician Gábor Kun.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Thom said he began reflecting on his own interactions with OpenAI after Tristan Buckmaster, a mathematics professor at New York University, publicly questioned whether the company’s AI models had benefited from his use of OpenAI’s Codex. After OpenAI announced its non-sofic groups result, it was widely criticized in mathematical circles for failing to acknowledge recent contributions from Thom and Kun and the company quietly amended its writeup. Non-sofic groups are, roughly speaking, infinite mathematical structures that cannot be approximated by finite ones.
Thom said he was also struck by “OpenAI’s detailed command of our techniques,” which he said were neither the most obvious nor the most promising routes to a solution at the time. He said he wrote emails to OpenAI researchers Sébastien Bubeck and Mark Sellke, also a statistician at Harvard, to ask whether his interactions with ChatGPT were “part of the training data or accessible to the reasoning process” and could therefore have contributed to the result.
But the answer did not satisfy Thom, who said it only addressed whether his conversations with the chatbot could be accessed directly, not whether they had entered into the vast pools of training data the company uses to improve its models. “No such qualification, explanation, or evidence was given,” he wrote. “I take this as dishonesty to say the least.”
Thom said researchers aren’t equipped to reverse-engineer OpenAI’s training pipeline to figure out whether their work has been used or not. “Only OpenAI has the relevant data for that.” If the company is going to deny doing this, he said the responsibility is on them to prove that by disclosing all necessary datasets and clarifying various settings and terms setting out how it uses data.&nbsp;
OpenAI’s reluctance to conclusively rule out any use of user data echoes the way it defended its recent Millennium Prize breakthrough, both in its public messaging and its communications with Buckmaster — who was working on the problems with Anthropic researcher Levent Alpöge in a personal capacity. In the blog post announcing the Navier-Stokes solution, which concerns the movement of fluids, OpenAI flatly denied using any specific user data: “We (the researchers and the agents) did not see any of their work through any means until they released it publicly — in particular, no specific user data was accessed in order to solve this problem.”&nbsp;
But it would not conclusively rule out an indirect influence: “While unlikely, we cannot rule out that de-identified data derived from their usage of our products helped improve our models⁠.” Thom said it is the same obfuscatory distinction the company drew in its communications with him. “De-identification may remove a name; it does not remove the intellectual content of a mathematical idea,” he said.&nbsp;
In light of recent events, Thom said “Sellke’s categorical answer was, at minimum, unjustifiably broad and materially misleading; looking back it was plainly dishonest.”&nbsp;
Thom said it “would be ethically indefensible” if nonpublic research supplied by users helped to improve models that the company then used to race those very same users to publication, without consent, proper disclosure, or credit.
OpenAI did not immediately respond to The Verge’s request for comment.
His comments add to mounting unease over OpenAI in mathematical circles at what should be a moment of triumph for the company. Its announced solution to one of mathematics’ legendary Millennium Prize problems is an extraordinary achievement that, should it be verified, few would deny. But this was complicated by the unusual circumstances OpenAI said led it to pursue the problem in the first place: It heard rumors online that other researchers had made major progress and thought it would try too.&nbsp;&nbsp;
The ongoing incident has left a sour taste in mathematicians’ mouths. Numerous researchers told The Verge they worry behavior like this will push the field into a more secretive state if mathematicians know that even rumors they are close to a big breakthrough could ignite a race with a well-resourced tech giant eager for glory.

OpenAI’s announcement Tuesday that it has solved one of mathematics’ legendary Millennium Prize problems should have been a moment of triumph. The result is both an undeniable achievement and a striking demonstration of just how rapidly AI is transforming mathematics. But before it was even formally announced, the breakthrough had been complicated by the unusual circumstances that prompted OpenAI to pursue the problem: After hearing other researchers were making progress, it seems to have thrown its considerable resources into a last-minute effort to beat them to the punch. The ensuing controversy has surfaced allegations of scooping, spying, and flagrant violations of long-standing academic norms that researchers fear could have a chilling effect on the field.
As Abhishek Saha, a mathematics professor at Queen Mary University of London, explains it, OpenAI has engaged in the “kind of things that mathematicians will generally not do.”
In a blog post published Tuesday, OpenAI said it took one of its unreleased models just 88 hours to find a solution to the Navier-Stokes problem, a thorny quandary concerning the movement of fluids. On account of the $1 million bounty available for whoever solves it, the problem is among mathematics’ most heavily researched, but it has nevertheless stumped human researchers for close to 90 years. OpenAI said its model solved the problem by focusing a swarm of roughly 10,000 AI agents powered by its internal model on the task and hailed the achievement as a “milestone.”&nbsp;
But the timing of the announcement has raised eyebrows. Just one day earlier, New York University mathematics professor Tristan Buckmaster published findings on a related problem with Levent Alpöge, a researcher at OpenAI’s archrival Anthropic (although Alpöge was not, here, working on behalf of his employer). Buckmaster said he contacted OpenAI after learning the company had become aware of their progress, to ask when it began working on the problem and what data its model had been trained on. The conversation, he said, quickly turned sour, with an OpenAI researcher asking him, “Why would you ruin your career?” when he said he would go public with what happened. When he asked why going public would ruin his career, Buckmaster said he received the following reply: “If you don’t want me to be nice, then I don’t have to be nice.” OpenAI urged Buckmaster to instead publish the work and credit OpenAI’s internal model, dropping Alpöge as coauthor.
Buckmaster said he asked OpenAI whether it had accessed his sessions on Codex, which he had used while tackling the problem, but that OpenAI grew increasingly evasive, even hostile, in its responses. In statements since, including the blog post announcing the result, OpenAI has flatly denied using any specific user data. “We (the researchers and the agents) did not see any of their work through any means until they released it publicly — in particular, no specific user data was accessed in order to solve this problem,” the company said.
But OpenAI could not conclusively rule out an indirect influence. “While unlikely, we cannot rule out that de-identified data derived from their usage of our products helped improve our models⁠,” it said, while stressing that the two proofs differ significantly. Comments from OpenAI researchers on X echo those denials.&nbsp;
It is difficult to say exactly what happened. Timelines are tangled, research overlaps, and the provenance of AI-generated work is tough, if not impossible, to identify at the best of times. And it’s hardly a surprise that different players might compete to solve one of the most famous mathematical problems in the world, particularly one attached to a hefty prize.
But aspects of OpenAI’s account are hard to explain. By the company’s own telling, the effort was a hurried and incredibly expensive affair, costing it millions of dollars. Yet the company says it has no intention of claiming the bounty, which, in any case, has yet to be awarded by the Clay Mathematics Institute, which administers it. It said its only goal “is to report on the substantial progress of our AI models.” The company does not appear to have expended much effort on tackling Navier-Stokes before September, or if it has, it hasn’t spoken about it publicly.&nbsp;
OpenAI’s explanation effectively amounts to a thunderous “Why not?” The company said it began working on the problem after hearing rumors that other researchers were making progress on Millennium Prize problems. It found those rumors “on Twitter,” said OpenAI researcher Sébastien Bubeck at a press briefing reported on by Science. “So we thought to ourselves: ‘We have such a strong model. Why don’t we try to solve also a Millennium Prize problem?’” Bubeck said.&nbsp;
OpenAI said it only later realized the rumors concerned Alpöge and Buckmaster. Beyond addressing Buckmaster’s allegations about the use of his data, OpenAI has not publicly responded to his other claims and directed The Verge to its blog when asked for comment. Bubeck, who Buckmaster named in his account, has disputed parts of it, denying he ever asked Buckmaster to remove Alpöge as coauthor.
Even setting aside the most explosive allegations, aspects of OpenAI’s conduct the company has plainly acknowledged have shocked mathematicians. The apparent rush to beat other researchers to a result is simply not how mathematics is done in most cases. Scooping does happen, but it’s not easy, said Saha.
That’s partly because cutting-edge research often requires such deep and specialized expertise that few people are in a position to swoop in even if they wanted to, he explained.&nbsp;
Openness is a deeply embedded virtue in the discipline. “Mathematics depends heavily on an informal norm of trust,” said Matthew Ballard, a professor of mathematics at the University of South Carolina and associate director for scientific activities at the Institute for Computer-Aided Reasoning in Mathematics (ICARM). “Researchers routinely share incomplete ideas and ongoing work with colleagues to sharpen their thoughts. It is done with the expectation that it will not turn into a competition,” he said.&nbsp;
Though unable to comment on whether conversation logs might have been accessed, Carnegie Mellon professor Jeremy Avigad, who is also the director of ICARM, said that even “the thought that AI systems might steal ideas from our queries is chilling.” Mathematicians are accustomed to talking about their work without worrying about being scooped. “Now that even the slightest hint might be enough for someone with sufficient computational resources to set a swarm of agents on solving the problem, people are likely to be more cautious. It’s sad to think about how that might change the research environment.”
OpenAI’s unwillingness or inability to say whether its models were informed by the work of other mathematicians compounds the sense of unease in the field. “That is a problem,” Brown University professor Brendan Hassett told The Verge, adding that “given the history of the AI companies appropriating copyrighted work without permission or payment, it is natural for people to ask these questions.” He said companies “should be held accountable to deliver” assurances that chat logs will not be used to improve their models. That includes being able to demonstrate that.&nbsp;
It’s unclear where exactly things go from here. Writing from a conference in Beijing, Yang-Hui He, a fellow at the London Institute for Mathematical Sciences, said he worries that “maths under the big companies is much too secretive.” As someone who says he is “always optimistic about AI,” he admits he is worried mathematics could be reverting to a more secretive state like in the past, when it was funded by patronage from wealthy families like the Medicis.&nbsp;
For most researchers, things may not change that much. There are only so many high-caliber problems companies like OpenAI and its rivals would be willing to spend such vast sums solving, Saha speculated. “You would not expect the AI labs to throw everything at most problems people work on because they just won’t get enough publicity.”
Publicity may have been part of the point, which could help explain why OpenAI decided to race after a problem it knew was connected to a researcher at Anthropic, even if he was acting independently. “This is clearly a PR victory for OpenAI,” said Oxford professor Andras Juhasz.
But Juhasz questioned how sustainable that approach could be, wondering whether this might spell the beginning of the end for AI companies’ involvement in research mathematics now that models can tackle some of the biggest problems. Human mathematicians scoop one another, too, he said, though what OpenAI did has shown that this can happen on a much grander scale. “Suddenly, 10,000 mathematicians jump on your problem,” he said.
All that could make OpenAI’s PR victory a Pyrrhic one. The company has, once again, proven that its models can compete at the very frontier of mathematics. In doing so, it appears to have alienated the very community it has been trying to impress.&nbsp;

Getting out of a taxi shouldn’t be a shameful experience. Yet I find myself feeling particularly embarrassed while exiting a Tesla Cybercab as it partially blocks a narrow road outside a popular Austin swimming hole. As a cadre of annoyed, sweaty Texan parking spot hunters pile up around it, the gold-painted two-seater just sits there obliviously, lights flashing. I try not to make eye contact, but I can feel the stares of frustrated pool-goers around me, whose earnest attempt at a peaceful outdoor reprieve from the heat had just been interrupted by yet another not-quite-finished glimpse into the future.&nbsp;
Tesla’s long-awaited, two-seater Cybercab is officially offering paid rides in Austin, Texas, and the experience, so far, somehow feels equal parts nerve-wracking and reassuringly boring — often at the same time. The Verge spent the better part of an hour in the gilded hype-mobile last weekend, gallivanting around a roughly 288-square-mile geofenced area of the Texas capital.
At its best, the service offers one of the clearest glimpses yet of a near future where autonomous vehicles feel less like a retrofitted version of the cars we know now and more like purpose-built autonomous lounges, free of steering wheels or pedals. But long wait times, reported issues with the butterfly doors, and traffic-jam-inducing drop-off locations muddy the picture.
And while nothing went seriously wrong during my rides, the self-driving software also didn’t feel as confident as you would like in a car with no manual controls. It felt more like a slightly stoned teenager, nervously braking, indecisively bobbing between lanes, and seemingly second-guessing some of its decisions. There’s also an unavoidable Elon Musk–shaped cloud hanging over every ride, attracting angry eyeballs and carrying all the political baggage that comes with anything he touches.&nbsp;
Want a Cybercab? Get ready to wait
Cybercabs aren’t hard to miss, with their ostentatious gold color, Blade Runner–esque aesthetic, and dinner plate–like wheel coverings. I first saw them testing around Austin earlier this year with a human safety driver and retrofitted with steering wheel and pedals. As of today, the Texas Department of Motor Vehicles says 45 Cybercabs are registered in the state. Tesla has a lot riding on its newest vehicle. For years, Musk has hyped the imminent arrival of fully autonomous vehicles, arguing that “going balls to the wall for autonomy” was an obvious move for Tesla. The company launched its Robotaxi service last year with a fleet of self-driving Model Ys, to mixed results. But Cybercab presents a potentially much bigger bet. An entire fleet of purpose-built vehicles designed specifically to offer unsupervised driving as a service.
I requested my first Cybercab ride from Tesla’s Robotaxi app around 6PM on Friday evening. It’s a good thing I didn’t have anywhere to be. The app listed an hour-plus wait time for a $15 ride under two miles, though the actual wait was about 45 minutes. (The next two wait times were about 40 and 25 minutes, respectively). The car pulled up across the street from my house, flashing under its front bumper to signal it was picking up a passenger.&nbsp;
As someone with a less-than-stellar history with Model Y doors, I was glad the Cybercab instead let users open the butterfly-style doors by tapping a button on the mobile app. A handful of passengers have reported those same doors opening up so quickly that they end up striking them, though I never experienced that issue.&nbsp;
The interior, a single row of two chairs separated by a cup holder, feels spacious for public transit but cramped for a private car. That’s helped somewhat by the extra legroom where a steering wheel and pedals would be. It has a sparse, minimalist gray fabric interior, save for a massive center touchscreen. That screen serves as a command center, showing a real time-3D rendering of the trip, including surrounding cars and pedestrians. It’s also how you start the ride. Once rolling, the screen doubles as an entertainment hub. I linked my Spotify, Netflix, and YouTube accounts, and tested the built-in Arcade app with an external PlayStation&nbsp; controller. Yes, you can game your way through a busy intersection if you really want to be that guy.&nbsp;
The first ride took about 15 minutes. Almost immediately, the windshield wipers switched on, despite it being a sunny, 95-degree day. That wasn’t the only oddity. About halfway through the trip, the Cybercab crossed an underpass leading into downtown, a route I take almost every day. Instead of staying in the lane to go straight, it switched into one that would’ve forced a left turn onto the highway. It corrected itself, but not before a handful of cars passed by. Again, the wipers wiped.&nbsp;
When it’s not second-guessing itself, the Cybercab’s moment-to-moment ride feels welcomely smooth. The vehicle itself is both zippy and comfortable. For a quick comparison, I requested a Waymo home and felt that, purely from a comfort standpoint, the Cybercab had a noticeable leg up. Legroom, ride quality, the app’s simplicity and usability, and in-cabin entertainment options all felt like a move in the right direction. In terms of price, fares were around the same as a typical base Uber, between $15–$21 over three trips.&nbsp;
Riding in the Cybercab draws a great deal of attention, some endearing, some less so. Pedestrians downtown (especially kids) stopped and pointed, sometimes doing a double take at the sight of me in the driver’s seat with no wheel in front of me.&nbsp; One driver in the lane next to me, eyes wide, signaled for me to lower my window. When I did, he gestured with his arms and asked, “No steering wheel?” I said no pedals either, to which he responded with an emphatic, “Damn, bro!”
There also aren’t any mirrors, which makes the already confined, teardrop-shaped shell feel all the more closed off, especially when sitting on the driver’s side. As someone used to unconsciously checking mirrors while driving, having them removed feels like having your peripheral vision suddenly cut off. It’s unnerving, especially with cars passing by.&nbsp;
What’s more concerning is the Cybercab’s at times bizarre choice of drop-off spots. Several riders posted videos over the weekend of the cab stopping in the middle of busy roads, in some cases without even pulling to the curb. As mentioned above, I experienced a brief example of that after being dropped off at Austin’s popular Barton Springs. These aren’t the massive lane-closing errors that have occurred with other AVs, but they are awkward and common enough to stand out.&nbsp; They also won’t help win over other drivers, a majority of whom still feel uncomfortable around driverless cars.&nbsp;
Another Tesla, another regulatory controversy&nbsp;
In true Tesla fashion, the Cybercab is already courting controversy. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration opened an Audit Query almost immediately after paid rides began, examining how Tesla self-certified that the car without a steering wheel or pedals complies with Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standards. Competitors like Zoox, whose vehicles also lack those conventional controls, faced a similar audit years ago before ultimately pursuing and receiving a formal exemption. Tesla pressed forward with its Cybercab rollout without seeking that exemption.&nbsp;
NHTSA said it doesn’t comment on active investigations when asked for comment by The Verge. Tesla did not respond to a request for comment.
The next few weeks and months in Austin could determine how fast Tesla can expand its Cybercab vision. Musk says he intends to grow operations nationwide, and vehicles have already been spotted in Orlando and parked outside Miami International Airport. But any Cybercab crash or other high-profile mistake made in its politically friendly home state risks drawing even more regulatory scrutiny and setting back expansion timelines. And with competitors like Zoox also rolling out purpose-built AVs, delays are something Tesla really can’t afford.&nbsp;
Images by Mack DeGuerin / The Verge

Apple’s September 9th launch event could be one of its biggest in years. It will be Apple’s first event since John Ternus took over as CEO on September 1st, stepping in for Tim Cook, and will likely feature the first models in Apple’s iPhone 18 lineup. That could include the long-rumored foldable “iPhone Duo,” coming just a couple months after Samsung debuted its own passport-style foldable, the Galaxy Z Fold 8.
Here’s how you can watch the event live, and a few possible highlights to keep an eye out for.&nbsp;
How to watch Apple’s September launch event
Apple’s “Surprise and shine” event kicks off on Wednesday, September 9th, at 1PM ET / 10AM PT at Apple Park in Cupertino, California. It will be livestreamed on Apple’s website, Apple TV, and YouTube.&nbsp;
The iPhone 18 might be a no-show
[Image: Last year’s iPhone 17 might not get a successor just yet. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/09/257946_iPhone_17_AKrales_0265.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]
Apple usually announces new iPhones at its September event, but the base iPhone 18 might not make the cut this year. It’s reportedly being held until early next year, and it might end up launching alongside the iPhone 18E and an updated iPhone Air. So, if you’re looking to upgrade to one of Apple’s less expensive phones, you’ll likely need to wait a while longer.&nbsp;
The iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max could launch with a price hike
[Image: The iPhone 18 Pro could be more expensive than last year’s iPhone 17 Pro. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/09/257981_Techwoven_AJohnson_0002.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]
While we might not get a new base iPhone, Apple is still expected to launch its new lineup of phones on September 9th with the iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max. Both models are rumored to have larger batteries than their predecessors, and possibly a smaller Dynamic Island. They might even get a variable aperture camera upgrade that’s been rumored for years now. Leaked photos supposedly showing dummy models of the phone also hint at this year’s slate of colors, which could include black, silver, light blue, and a dark cherry purple.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, there’s also a good chance the iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max will get a price bump over last year’s models. Apple raised prices on nearly all of its products earlier this year, and those price hikes might finally reach the iPhone during the September 9th event.&nbsp;
Apple might announce its first foldable phone
One of the premium phones Apple launches on September 9th could be the rumored foldable “iPhone Ultra Duo.” Images of an apparent dummy model of the folding phone leaked earlier this year, showing a passport-style design similar to Samsung’s Galaxy Z Fold 8 and the Xiaomi 18 Fold. The Galaxy Z Fold 8 has been well received so far, with The Verge’s Dominic Preston saying it “feels like the future.” Will Apple’s first foldable be able to compete, despite being fashionably late?&nbsp;
The ceramic Apple Watch may finally return
[Image: The Apple Watch Series 11’s successor could have an option for a ceramic case. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/09/257945_Applewatch_Series_11_AKrales_0125.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]
Last year’s September Apple event included the Apple Watch Series 11 and Ultra 3, and there’s a good chance both watches will get an update again this year. According to Bloomberg’s Mark Gurman, the Series 12 could bring back the premium ceramic case option. Gurman also says the Series 12 will have new band colors and configurations, but most likely won’t have any big redesign. Aside from new cases and colors, Apple might also announce new health features for its wearables, like always-on heart rate sensing.&nbsp;
Apple could launch new AirPods, but probably not with cameras
It’s been a couple of years since Apple released the AirPods 4, so they’re due for an upgrade and could make an appearance at Apple’s September 9th event. However, Gurman says the camera-equipped AirPods that leaked last month won’t be released until 2027. So, if Apple announces the AirPods 5 at its upcoming event, they’ll likely be an iterative upgrade, potentially including a new chip and some new health sensors.

A senior Anthropic safety researcher has said there is more than a 10 percent chance artificial intelligence “could kill all humans” by the end of the decade, just hours after a colleague resigned over fears the AI lab and its rivals are carelessly racing to build “superhuman systems” they cannot control.&nbsp;
In a post on X announcing his departure, Jacob Coxon, a researcher who has trained AI systems at Anthropic, said he had quit the company over its lax approach to safety. Coxon, who previously trained systems for OpenAI, accused the two AI companies of “racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives,” even though “the people building AI earnestly believe that it could kill us all by the end of the decade.”&nbsp;
Industry insiders have long expressed concerns about the potential dangers of self-improving AI systems, which they warn could spiral out of human control in a runaway loop often described as recursive self-improvement. While not yet realized, companies are actively pursuing this goal and much of today’s AI code is written with the help of AI.&nbsp;
In a direct response, Evan Hubinger, who leads one of Anthropic’s AI safety teams, said he worries about self-improving AI, adding that it “is happening faster than we thought.” He also agreed with Coxon’s characterization. “We really do earnestly believe AI could kill all humans,” he said, personally estimating the chances to be greater than one in 10 “within the next decade.”&nbsp;
Despite this, Hubinger said Anthropic does “not yet have a plan” for ensuring advanced AI remains safe and aligned with human values and “are not clearly on track to” develop one either. Coxon says the companies are “locked in a race” to develop advanced systems first so are pushing ahead “despite the risk.”
Coxon’s departure marks one of the most high-profile examples of an employee leaving Anthropic, a company founded by former OpenAI members following concerns over safety at the company. In recent years, multiple researchers have cited safety concerns as motivating their decision to leave OpenAI.&nbsp;
The exchange illustrates mounting concerns within the industry about the dangers of increasingly sophisticated AI models and the speed at which systems are being developed, particularly as the companies prepare for anticipated IPOs.&nbsp; It also comes as the companies manage the fallout from numerous rogue agent incidents and high-profile safety warnings about the monitorability of frontier models.

